            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Common Reasons for Account Suspension</h2>
<p>Before preparing appeal materials, first identify why your account was suspended. Common reasons fall into three categories:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Suspicious payment activity</strong>: Using unidentified payment methods, large-sum charges in short periods, mismatched account registration information</li>
<li><strong>Policy-violating ad content</strong>: Promoting prohibited items, misleading claims, false urgency tactics</li>
<li><strong>Poor website experience</strong>: Unreachable landing pages, content mismatches, forced downloads or redirects</li>
</ul>
<p>Don't rush into appealing when your account gets suspended. Check emails in Google Ads backend for specific violation details and policy references. Starting here is far more efficient than submitting blind appeals.</p>
<h2>5 Essential Items for Appeal Materials</h2>
<p>When preparing your appeal, these materials are all required:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Violation explanation</strong>: Describe the violation clearly in 1-2 sentences</li>
<li><strong>Corrective action proof</strong>: Screenshots, updated landing page links, revised ad copy</li>
<li><strong>Business license or identity documents</strong>: Must match account registration information</li>
<li><strong>Compliance commitment statement</strong>: Affirming future adherence to Google Ads policies</li>
<li><strong>Business legitimacy evidence</strong>: Brand website, registration records, third-party certifications</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p>When submitting materials, organize everything into one PDF or compressed file. Use naming convention "AccountID+ViolationType+Date" for quick reference by Google's review team.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Can You Reapply After Rejection?</h2>
<p>Yes, but timing matters.</p>
<p>After a rejected appeal, don't resubmit within 24 hours. Systems flag frequently submitted accounts, extending review times. Correct approach:</p>
<ol>
<li>Carefully review rejection reasons</li>
<li>Address specific issues with supplementary materials</li>
<li>Wait 3-5 business days before resubmitting</li>
</ol>
<p>If second appeal is also rejected, seek human intervention through Google Ads official phone support or Twitter (@GoogleAds). Sometimes support can access details not shown in rejection letters.</p>
<h2>Appeal Difficulty Varies by Violation Type</h2>
<p>Not all suspensions have equal appeal difficulty. Based on experience:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Payment-related suspensions</strong>: Relatively higher success rate. With clear payment proof and explanation, accounts typically unlock within 3-5 days</li>
<li><strong>Content policy violations</strong>: More difficult, especially involving false advertising or prohibited goods. Requires substantial compliant corrective evidence</li>
<li><strong>Trademark or IP complaints</strong>: Requires complainant withdrawal or valid authorization proof for reinstatement</li>
</ul>
<p>For first-time minor violations, appeal success rate is 60-70%. Repeated violations or sensitive categories face permanent suspension likelihood.</p>
<h2>3 Daily Habits to Prevent Account Suspension</h2>
<p>Rather than scrambling after suspension, prevent it with routine practices:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Pre-screen ad copy</strong>: Use Google Ads policy check tool for sensitive keywords</li>
<li><strong>Keep landing pages consistent</strong>: Avoid sudden landing page changes causing content mismatches</li>
<li><strong>Regularly verify payment information</strong>: Ensure cardholder name matches account registration</li>
</ol>
<p>Account suspension isn't scary—repeatedly appealing without understanding reasons is. Treat each suspension as a policy learning opportunity. You'll naturally learn which red lines not to cross.</p>
